.\" cpu-power(1) -- GENERATED from packaging/cpu-power.1.in by CMake.
.\" The installed paths below are substituted from the same build variables the program is
.\" compiled with, so the manual cannot describe a layout the binary does not use.
.TH CPU\-POWER 1 "2026-09-16" "cpu-power @PROJECT_VERSION@" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
cpu\-power \- adjust CPU frequency scaling, turbo and audio latency from a window
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cpu\-power
.RB [ \-\-scale
.IR factor ]
.br
.B cpu\-power \-\-help
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cpu\-power
is a small window for the CPU power settings the Linux kernel exposes under
.IR /sys/devices/system/cpu .
It sets the scaling governor, the energy/performance preference, the frequency
limits, turbo, and the system's C\-state exit latency limit.
.PP
It is aimed at the case where the machine boots conservatively and some particular
piece of work \- rendering, compiling, recording audio \- wants it to stop being
conservative for a while, after which it should go back to how it was.
.PP
.B Nothing is applied until the Active toggle is turned on,
and turning it off puts everything back. So does closing the window, and so does the
program being killed: the privileged helper restores the machine when its input closes,
which happens whatever kills the window. There is no path that depends on an orderly
shutdown.
.PP
.B The tool does not know what your CPU can do; it asks.
Governor names are read from
.IR scaling_available_governors ,
preference names from
.IR energy_performance_available_preferences ,
frequency limits from
.IR cpuinfo_min_freq " and " cpuinfo_max_freq ,
and turbo from whichever of
.IR intel_pstate/no_turbo ", " cpufreq/boost " or " policyX/boost
this machine has. A driver it has never heard of still works. A control the machine does
not offer is shown disabled and labelled, never silently ignored, and the readout shows
what the kernel reports afterwards rather than what was asked for.
.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-scale " factor"
Scale the window. Useful on a high\-DPI display. The value is remembered, so this is
normally needed once.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print usage, including where preferences are stored, and exit.
.
.SH THE CONTROLS
.TP
.B The slider
Five stops \- Powersave, Balanced, Balanced\-Performance, Performance, Maximum.
They are named rather than numbered because the kernel's knobs are enumerations and
there is no continuum between them. Each stop is a
.I wish
resolved against what the running driver actually offers, so the same stop means the
same thing on machines that implement it completely differently: where
.B intel_pstate
is active the top four stops are separated by the energy/performance preference, and
where it is not they are separated by the governor. The readout shows which.
.TP
.B Active
The master toggle, and the tool's whole safety property. Off means the machine is
exactly as the kernel left it. It is also what starts the privileged helper \- nothing
runs as root until this is turned on.
.TP
.B Turbo
Whether the CPU may clock above its rated base frequency.
.TP
.B DAW mode
Caps C\-state exit latency, which is a different problem from clock speed and is not
solved by any governor setting. A core that finishes early goes idle, and waking it from
a deep C\-state costs hundreds of microseconds out of an audio period that may only be
about 1300 of them \- so a faster core can produce
.I more
dropouts, not fewer. This holds
.I /dev/cpu_dma_latency
open at the smallest non\-zero exit latency the machine reports: the shallowest state
that still halts the core. It does not request zero, which would admit only the POLL
pseudo\-state and leave every core spinning for no benefit. Because the kernel drops the
constraint when the last descriptor closes, DAW mode cannot get stuck on.
.
.SH HOW IT REACHES ROOT
The window is never root and is never setuid. One small program, installed at
.IR @CPUPOWER_HELPER_PATH@ ,
runs as root, and it is reached by the least intrusive route available:
.PP
.RS
.nf
1.  already root       run it directly
2.  pkexec             but only if polkit ALREADY authorises
                       @CPUPOWER_POLKIT_ACTION_ID@ with no interaction
3.  neither            fail, saying what to do about it
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B This program never asks for a password and never handles one,
and step 2 is what guarantees it rather than merely intending it. Before using
.BR pkexec (1)
the program asks
.BR pkcheck (1)
whether the action will be authorised silently, and proceeds only on an unqualified yes.
A missing or unparseable action does not make
.B pkexec
fail \- it makes it fall back to
.IR org.freedesktop.policykit.exec ,
whose defaults are
.IR auth_admin ,
and the session's own authentication agent turns that into a root password dialog. The
pre\-flight question cannot itself prompt, because
.B \-\-allow\-user\-interaction
is never passed.
.PP
On the normal path nothing is asked at all: the installed action's defaults are
.IR allow_any=no ", " allow_inactive=no ", " allow_active=yes ,
which grants the request to whoever is
.I physically at the machine
and to nobody else \- not a remote login, not a switched\-away session, not another
logged\-in user. Adjusting your own CPU is a preference, not an administrative act, and
this is how every desktop settings panel treats it.
.PP
There is no
.B sudo
fallback and no interactive fallback. A
.BR sudoers (5)
rule would be a
.I broader
grant than the polkit action, not a narrower one, because it also applies over SSH and
from any process that user can start. Without polkit, run the helper as root yourself;
the program prints the exact command when it cannot reach it.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I @CPUPOWER_HELPER_PATH@
The privileged helper. Plain C, libc only, no configuration file, no network, no shell.
It takes no path from anyone: it opens one descriptor on
.I /sys/devices/system/cpu
at startup, proves it is sysfs, and anchors every later access to it.
.TP
.I @CPUPOWER_POLKIT_ACTIONDIR@/@CPUPOWER_POLKIT_ACTION_ID@.policy
The polkit action \- the rule saying who may run the helper. If this is missing or
cannot be parsed, polkit ignores it silently and the tool falls back.
.TP
.I ~/.config/cpu-power/config
Remembered window preferences. It records where the controls sit and
.B is not applied at startup;
nothing changes until Active is turned on.
Honours
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME .
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal exit.
.TP
.B 1
Could not start: no display, or the CPU interface could not be read.
.TP
.B 2
Bad command line.
.
.SH SECURITY
.B pkexec is setuid root.
That is a real cost of reaching privilege this way, and it is stated rather than glossed.
What it buys is that no part of this project ever handles your password. The install sets
no setuid or setgid bit of its own.
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pkexec (1),
.BR polkit (8),
.BR sudoers (5)
.PP
The kernel's own documentation is the authority for everything this tool writes:
.IR Documentation/admin\-guide/pm/cpufreq.rst ,
.IR intel_pstate.rst ,
.IR cpuidle.rst " and " pm_qos_interface.rst .
